<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Iberia</p><h1 class="st-title">Arevaci</h1><p class="st-meta">400 BCE – 133 BCE (approx.) · capital Numantia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The strongest of the Celtiberian peoples, defenders of Numantia, whose siege by Scipio Aemilianus in 134 to 133 BCE became a byword for resistance.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Arevaci occupied the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/douro">Douro</a> basin in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soria">Soria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segovia">Segovia</a> from about the 4th century BCE. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> called them the most powerful of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/celtiberians">Celtiberians</a>, and their towns included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numantia">Numantia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiermes">Termes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uxama-argaela">Uxama</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>They lived in large fortified towns with grid-like streets, governed by councils and elected war leaders. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numantia">Numantia's</a> pottery shows painted horses and warriors. Herding, cereal growing and iron working sustained them, and their warriors were renowned in Roman sources for courage and skill with the sword.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From 153 BCE they led resistance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, defeating several consuls. In 134 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-aemilianus">Scipio Aemilianus</a> surrounded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numantia">Numantia</a> with walls and starved it; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/retogenes">Retogenes</a> broke out to seek aid, but the town fell in 133 BCE. The Arevaci later backed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quintus-sertorius">Sertorius</a> before becoming part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/hispania-citerior">Hispania Citerior</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="tribal confederation, 400 BCE – 133 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">tribal confederation</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Iberia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:51.74%;width:5.31%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>400 BCE – 133 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Numantia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Avarus, Retogenes</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Celtic polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Celtiberian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Arevacians</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-266&amp;focus=arevaci" aria-label="Arevaci on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4595 698 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Arevaci lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4915 954L5031 967"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5031 967h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4915 954h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4915 954h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-266&amp;focus=arevaci">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
